  {
   "id": "return",
   "title": "Return and compounding",
   "minutes": 2,
   "body": [
    "A **backtest** replays a set of trading rules over past prices and records what the account would have been worth each day. The first number everyone looks at is the return.",
    "Parity shows return per year as **CAGR**: the steady yearly growth rate that would turn the starting money into the final money. It is not the average of the yearly returns. Bad years cost more than good years earn, so the steady rate is lower than the simple average whenever returns jump around.",
    "Small differences in yearly return grow into large differences over decades, because each year's gain also earns a return. That is compounding. In the picture, 10% a year ends more than 7 times higher than 3% a year after 30 years. It is why a return is only comparable with another return over the same dates and at similar risk."
   ],
   "terms": [
    "backtest",
    "cagr",
    "compounding",
    "return"
   ],
