     "q": "What does CAGR measure?",
     "options": [
      "The steady yearly growth rate that gets from the start value to the end value",
      "The average of the daily returns",
      "The best single year"
     ],
     "answer": 0,
     "why": "CAGR is the final value over the start value, to the power one over the years, minus one. It is a steady rate, not a best year or a simple average."
    },
    {
     "q": "Why can two strategies with the same average yearly return end with different amounts?",
     "options": [
      "Because of rounding",
      "Because bumpier returns compound to less",
      "They cannot"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "why": "Losses cost more than gains of the same size earn back. The bumpier path compounds to less, which is called volatility drag."
    },
