  {
   "id": "sharpe",
   "title": "The Sharpe ratio: return per bump",
   "minutes": 2,
   "body": [
    "The **Sharpe ratio** divides return by volatility. It answers: how much did this earn for each unit of bumpiness? That makes strategies with different risk levels comparable.",
    "A strategy earning 8% with 8% volatility has a Sharpe of 1. One earning 8% with 16% volatility has a Sharpe of 0.5: the same gain, twice the ride. The picture shows both. Around 0.5 is what the stock market has done. Above 1 over many years is rare.",
    "The Sharpe ratio hides some things. It treats a sudden crash the same as many small dips, so a strategy with lots of small wins and rare big losses can look better than it is. Read it next to the worst fall."
   ],
